    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Religious liberal frameworks often rest on the theology of the \"sacred death\" (particularly in Catholic social teaching and some Protestant traditions), which holds that the end of life is natural and that the physician has no authority to end a life on the patient's request.\n\nThis position is frequently supported by the \"double effect\" principle and the belief that the ultimate authority over whether one endures life and death lies with the individual patient (often encouraged to consult their conscience and physician).\n\nThis approach relies heavily on the concepts of human dignity and the right to peace, arguing that a \"dignified\" death as a natural part of the human journey is better than being forced to \"drift\" on a life-sustaining machine that the patient may no longer wish to support.",
